const lesson = (teach, operate, example, memory) => ({ teach, operate, example, memory });

export const lessons = {
  aws: [
    lesson('AWS IAM decides who may perform which action on which resource. Prefer short-lived role credentials and least-privilege policies; avoid long-lived access keys and broad wildcards.', 'Evaluate identity policies, resource policies, permission boundaries, SCPs, and explicit denies together. Verify with IAM Access Analyzer, policy simulation, and CloudTrail rather than guessing.', 'A marketplace gives its image processor permission to read one upload prefix and write one derivative prefix instead of granting the whole S3 account.', 'Identity, action, resource, conditions, then explicit deny.'),
    lesson('A VPC is a private routing boundary containing subnets, route tables, gateways, security groups, and network ACLs. Public IP addressing alone does not make a subnet public; a working route to an internet gateway does.', 'Trace packets hop by hop: DNS, source route, security group, NACL, destination route, and return path. Use VPC Flow Logs and Reachability Analyzer, and keep application tiers in private subnets.', 'An online marketplace exposes only its load balancer while application and database subnets have no direct inbound internet route.', 'A subnet is public because of its route, not its name.'),
    lesson('EC2 provides machine-level control; Lambda runs bounded event-driven functions without server management. Choose from workload duration, startup sensitivity, scaling shape, runtime control, and total cost—not fashion.', 'For EC2, test autoscaling, health replacement, patching, and capacity limits. For Lambda, bound concurrency, retries, timeouts, cold-start impact, and downstream connection pressure.', 'Campaign image resizing runs on Lambda with reserved concurrency, while a latency-sensitive long-running recommendation process stays on autoscaled EC2.', 'Lambda removes server ownership, not workload limits.'),
    lesson('S3 is a highly durable object store, not a POSIX filesystem. Design around object keys, whole-object writes, versioning, lifecycle rules, encryption, and explicit bucket access.', 'Block public access, prefer role-based access, enable versioning for recovery, and monitor 4xx/5xx errors. Diagnose wrong-region requests, policy denies, KMS permissions, and hot-key assumptions with request evidence.', 'Marketplace product images use immutable versioned keys, CloudFront delivery, lifecycle transition for originals, and a separate private upload prefix.', 'Objects have keys and versions; directories are only prefixes.'),
    lesson('RDS fits relational joins, constraints, and multi-row transactions; DynamoDB fits known key-based access patterns at predictable scale. Model the access pattern first because DynamoDB cannot rescue an undefined query model.', 'Protect either database with encryption, backups, restore tests, alarms, and bounded clients. For RDS watch locks, connections, replicas, and query plans; for DynamoDB watch partition distribution, throttling, and conditional writes.', 'Orders and payments remain in PostgreSQL on RDS, while idempotency records and campaign counters use DynamoDB conditional writes with TTL.', 'Choose by access and consistency needs, then operate the failure modes.'),
  ],
  azure: [
    lesson('Microsoft Entra ID authenticates identities; Azure RBAC authorizes actions on Azure resources. Use managed identities for workloads and scoped role assignments instead of storing client secrets.', 'Review inherited assignments from management group to resource, use Privileged Identity Management for elevated access, and inspect sign-in and activity logs. A token can be valid yet lack the required data-plane role.', 'A corporate application uses a managed identity to read one Key Vault and employees use Entra groups mapped to application roles.', 'Authenticate the identity; authorize the resource action.'),
    lesson('Azure Virtual Networks provide address and routing boundaries through subnets, route tables, NSGs, private endpoints, peering, and gateways. Peering connects networks but is not automatically transitive.', 'Trace DNS resolution, effective routes, NSG rules, private endpoint state, and return paths. Use Network Watcher connection troubleshoot and flow logs before changing rules.', 'An enterprise API reaches Azure SQL through a private endpoint while only Application Gateway has an internet-facing listener.', 'DNS chooses the destination; routes and NSGs decide whether packets arrive.'),
    lesson('App Service hosts web applications with managed platform operations; Functions runs event-driven units that scale by triggers. Choose by request lifecycle, scaling behavior, deployment needs, and dependency limits.', 'Use deployment slots, health checks, managed identity, application settings, and explicit scale limits. For Functions, control retries, poison messages, timeout, cold starts, and downstream connections.', 'The employee portal runs on App Service; document conversion runs from a queue-triggered Function with idempotency and a dead-letter path.', 'App Service hosts an app; Functions reacts to events.'),
    lesson('Azure Storage separates blob, queue, file, and table workloads. Use the service whose consistency and access model matches the data, and prefer private endpoints plus Entra authorization over account keys.', 'Enable versioning or soft delete where recovery matters, rotate any unavoidable keys, and inspect storage diagnostics for authentication, throttling, and latency. Design lifecycle and redundancy from recovery objectives.', 'HR uploads land in a private Blob container, trigger scanning through Queue Storage, and move to a cool tier after the retention window.', 'Pick the storage behavior first, then the redundancy tier.'),
    lesson('Azure SQL provides relational constraints and transactions; Cosmos DB provides globally distributed, partitioned document access with selectable consistency. Cosmos design begins with partition key and request-unit economics.', 'For Azure SQL monitor query plans, blocking, connections, geo-replication, and restore drills. For Cosmos monitor hot partitions, RU throttling, consistency choice, indexes, and multi-region failover.', 'Payroll remains in Azure SQL, while a globally read employee directory uses Cosmos DB partitioned by tenant with bounded-staleness reads.', 'Relational truth or partitioned global access: decide before choosing the product.'),
  ],
  gcp: [
    lesson('Google Cloud IAM binds principals to roles on resources. Prefer predefined roles, workload identity, and service-account impersonation; avoid service-account keys and project-wide primitive roles.', 'Trace organization policies, inherited bindings, conditions, and deny policies. Use Policy Troubleshooter, audit logs, and IAM Recommender to prove access and safely reduce it.', 'A Cloud Run catalog service impersonates a narrowly scoped service account to read one bucket without shipping a JSON key.', 'Principal plus role plus resource, constrained by policy.'),
    lesson('A Google Cloud VPC is global, while subnets are regional. Routes, hierarchical firewall policies, Cloud NAT, load balancers, private service access, and DNS determine connectivity.', 'Check name resolution, effective routes, firewall rules, NAT ports, load-balancer health, and return traffic. Connectivity Tests and VPC Flow Logs provide evidence before rule changes.', 'Global users reach an external HTTPS load balancer; regional Cloud Run and database traffic stay on private paths.', 'Global network, regional subnets, explicit paths.'),
    lesson('Cloud Run is managed request- or event-driven container compute; GKE gives Kubernetes control and ecosystem flexibility. Cloud Run is simpler until scheduling, networking, sidecars, or platform policy truly require a cluster.', 'For Cloud Run bound instances, concurrency, timeout, cold starts, and database connections. For GKE own upgrades, requests, limits, autoscaling, disruption, security, and cluster observability.', 'A stateless global read API runs on Cloud Run; a GPU inference platform with custom scheduling and service mesh runs on GKE.', 'Start with managed containers; buy cluster control only when required.'),
    lesson('Cloud Storage holds immutable-style objects with classes, lifecycle, retention, encryption, and IAM. It is not a shared disk and object names do not create real directories.', 'Use uniform bucket-level access, versioning or retention where required, signed URLs for bounded sharing, and audit logs. Diagnose policy, KMS, location, and lifecycle behavior from logs.', 'A media service stores originals under immutable keys, delivers through Cloud CDN, and expires temporary renditions automatically.', 'Object key, access policy, lifecycle, recovery.'),
    lesson('Cloud SQL provides managed relational databases; Spanner provides horizontally scalable relational semantics and synchronous replication. Spanner is justified by scale and availability requirements, not by a desire to avoid schema design.', 'Cloud SQL needs connection pooling, query tuning, replicas, backups, and failover drills. Spanner needs good primary-key distribution, transaction awareness, instance sizing, and latency measurement across regions.', 'Account billing stays on regional Cloud SQL, while a truly global ledger with strict consistency and proven scale uses Spanner.', 'Pay for global consistency only when the requirement proves it.'),
  ],
  'javascript-typescript': [
    lesson('JavaScript runs one call stack per agent while the host schedules timers, I/O, and events. Promise callbacks enter the microtask queue, which drains before the next task and can starve rendering or I/O if abused.', 'Keep CPU-heavy work off the main or event-loop thread, measure event-loop delay, and split or move computation to workers. Never assume asynchronous means parallel.', 'A Node API streams network responses efficiently but moves image transformation to worker threads so one request cannot freeze every connection.', 'Stack now, microtasks next, tasks later.'),
    lesson('TypeScript checks static relationships before runtime; it does not validate JSON, database rows, or user input. Use narrow domain types internally and runtime validation at every untrusted boundary.', 'Avoid unchecked assertions and duplicated server/client types. Use discriminated unions for state, strict compiler settings, and type tests around reusable contracts.', 'A shared order type improves refactoring, while a runtime schema still rejects a malformed webhook before it reaches business logic.', 'Types protect code paths; validators protect boundaries.'),
    lesson('A Promise represents one eventual result. Use async/await for readable sequencing, Promise.all for independent work, allSettled for independent partial outcomes, and cancellation signals when abandoned work matters.', 'Handle rejection exactly once, bound concurrency, propagate AbortSignal, and avoid fire-and-forget work in request handlers. Measure queueing and dependency latency separately.', 'A product page fetches price and inventory concurrently but limits calls across a large catalog and cancels them when navigation changes.', 'Sequence dependencies; parallelize independence; cancel irrelevance.'),
    lesson('ES modules create explicit static imports and exports that tools can analyze. Keep module boundaries cohesive, avoid cycles and hidden side effects, and publish package exports rather than internal paths.', 'Check runtime format, resolution rules, tree shaking, and initialization order. A circular import may expose partially initialized bindings even when the type checker is happy.', 'A browser and Node application share pure domain modules while framework adapters remain separate entry points.', 'Dependencies point inward; side effects stay at the edge.'),
    lesson('A useful JavaScript test checks observable behavior at the cheapest reliable boundary. Unit-test pure rules, integrate real serializers and databases where contracts matter, and reserve browser tests for critical journeys.', 'Control time and randomness, avoid implementation-detail mocks, test rejected promises and cancellation, and make failures deterministic. Coverage is a map, not proof of correctness.', 'Checkout calculation has fast table-driven unit tests, a database integration test, and one browser purchase path instead of hundreds of brittle UI mocks.', 'Test behavior, boundary, failure, then journey.'),
  ],
  python: [
    lesson('Python names reference objects; mutability, identity, equality, iteration protocols, and data-model methods determine behavior. Copying a container is shallow unless nested objects are explicitly copied.', 'Use immutable values for stable keys and boundaries, dataclasses for clear records, and profile allocations before optimizing. Watch mutable defaults and accidental aliasing.', 'A pricing service uses frozen value objects for money and avoids sharing a mutable default list across requests.', 'Names point to objects; assignment does not copy.'),
    lesson('Python type hints are optional static contracts consumed by tools; they do not enforce runtime input. Use precise public annotations, protocols for behavior, and validation where external data enters.', 'Run a strict checker in CI, narrow Optional values, avoid Any leakage, and keep runtime schemas separate from type-only models when trust differs.', 'A data API validates incoming JSON with a runtime model and exposes a typed protocol so storage adapters can be changed safely.', 'Hints guide developers; validators reject bad data.'),
    lesson('asyncio gives cooperative concurrency on one event loop. It helps many waiting I/O operations, not CPU-bound work, and every blocking call on the loop delays unrelated tasks.', 'Use structured task ownership, timeouts, cancellation, bounded semaphores, and async-compatible libraries. Move CPU work to processes or a dedicated worker system.', 'A service concurrently calls several APIs with per-call timeouts and a global concurrency cap, while video encoding runs in worker processes.', 'Async hides waiting, not computation.'),
    lesson('Python packaging defines build metadata, dependencies, importable modules, and reproducible installation. Use pyproject.toml, pinned application environments, isolated builds, and a deliberate public package surface.', 'Separate runtime and development dependencies, scan the resolved lock, test the built wheel, and avoid imports that depend on the working directory.', 'A data service builds one wheel in CI and promotes the same artifact through environments instead of rebuilding from an unpinned requirements file.', 'Build once; resolve explicitly; import predictably.'),
    lesson('Python tests should make state and dependencies visible. Use small unit tests for rules, fixtures for explicit setup, integration tests for adapters, and contract tests for external assumptions.', 'Freeze clocks only where needed, avoid giant shared fixtures, test exceptions and retries, and keep network calls out of unit suites. Use property tests when invariants matter more than examples.', 'A payment parser uses table tests for formats, property checks for round trips, and a sandbox integration test for provider compatibility.', 'Arrange visible state; assert behavior and failure.'),
  ],
  java: [
    lesson('The JVM loads bytecode, manages heap objects, compiles hot paths, and collects unreachable memory. Latency problems can come from allocation rate, pauses, locks, native memory, or poor code—not simply “too little heap.”', 'Use GC logs, Java Flight Recorder, heap histograms, and thread dumps before tuning. Set container-aware limits and investigate retained objects rather than raising memory indefinitely.', 'A transaction service finds an unbounded cache retaining objects through a heap dump instead of masking it with a larger pod limit.', 'Measure allocation, retention, pause, and native memory separately.'),
    lesson('Java collections encode ordering, uniqueness, lookup, and concurrency guarantees. Choose from required operations and complexity; program to interfaces but understand the concrete implementation.', 'Define equality and hashCode consistently, avoid mutable map keys, size collections when evidence supports it, and use concurrent collections instead of unsynchronized shared HashMaps.', 'An idempotency service uses ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent for local coordination and a database constraint for cross-instance truth.', 'Choose the guarantee before the collection.'),
    lesson('Java concurrency coordinates threads through executors, futures, locks, atomics, and structured ownership. Shared mutable state creates visibility and race problems unless a happens-before relationship exists.', 'Bound executors and queues, propagate cancellation and deadlines, avoid blocking common pools, and inspect thread dumps plus saturation metrics. Prefer immutable messages and isolated ownership.', 'A transaction service uses a bounded executor for partner calls and rejects excess work rather than building an unlimited queue that exhausts memory.', 'Threads are finite; queues are promises about memory and latency.'),
    lesson('Streams express transformations over data; they are lazy until a terminal operation and are not automatically faster. Use them when the pipeline is clearer than a loop, not to hide stateful side effects.', 'Avoid parallel streams for blocking I/O or unknown common-pool workloads, preserve encounter-order only when needed, and profile allocation-heavy chains.', 'A report service uses a readable stream pipeline for filtering and grouping, but a simple loop for a hot allocation-sensitive parser.', 'Streams optimize clarity first; measure performance.'),
    lesson('Java testing should separate domain behavior from framework wiring. Use plain unit tests for rules, focused integration tests for persistence and serialization, and a few end-to-end paths.', 'Avoid mocking value objects and every collaborator, control clocks and executors, test transaction rollback and concurrency invariants, and use containers only where the real engine matters.', 'Ledger rules run in milliseconds without Spring; a PostgreSQL integration test proves locking behavior that an in-memory database cannot reproduce.', 'Test the rule plainly and the infrastructure honestly.'),
  ],
  csharp: [
    lesson('C# distinguishes value and reference semantics, nullable state, generics, records, and pattern matching. Model invalid states out where practical, but still validate external input at runtime.', 'Avoid accidental boxing, mutable shared records, and null-forgiving operators that silence real uncertainty. Keep domain values explicit and benchmark hot generic paths.', 'A workflow service models each state as a discriminated-style record hierarchy so impossible transitions cannot be represented casually.', 'Represent state precisely; validate trust boundaries.'),
    lesson('LINQ composes queries, but execution may be deferred and the provider decides what can run remotely. An IEnumerable pipeline is in-process; IQueryable may become SQL and has different performance and translation limits.', 'Materialize deliberately, project only required fields, avoid N+1 navigation access, inspect generated SQL, and never enumerate an expensive query repeatedly.', 'An admin report projects directly to a DTO in SQL instead of loading entire entities and filtering them in memory.', 'Know who executes the query and when.'),
    lesson('async/await frees a thread while an operation waits; it does not make CPU work cheaper. Propagate Task and CancellationToken, avoid sync-over-async, and use bounded parallelism.', 'Observe all task failures, set deadlines, avoid Task.Run around naturally asynchronous I/O, and monitor thread-pool starvation plus dependency latency.', 'A workflow API awaits database and HTTP I/O, passes the request cancellation token, and limits concurrent partner calls.', 'Async releases waiting threads; cancellation releases abandoned work.'),
    lesson('.NET uses managed garbage collection, but unmanaged handles, large objects, pins, and retained references still require design. IDisposable expresses deterministic release of scarce resources.', 'Use using/await using, pool only measured hot resources, inspect allocation profiles and dumps, and avoid finalizers unless wrapping unmanaged ownership correctly.', 'A document service streams uploads and disposes each stream promptly instead of buffering every file on the large object heap.', 'GC handles memory reachability; IDisposable handles scarce ownership.'),
    lesson('C# tests should keep domain logic independent from ASP.NET and EF Core where possible. Unit-test rules, integrate the real database for translation and transactions, and exercise authorization at the HTTP boundary.', 'Avoid mocking IQueryable, test cancellation and concurrency, use deterministic clocks, and assert externally visible results rather than private calls.', 'A multi-step workflow has fast state-transition tests plus a PostgreSQL integration test proving its optimistic-concurrency behavior.', 'Plain domain tests, real boundary tests.'),
  ],
  go: [
    lesson('A goroutine is a lightweight concurrently scheduled function managed by the Go runtime. It is useful for independent waiting work, but it does not remove synchronization, cancellation, backpressure, or ownership responsibilities.', 'Every goroutine needs a clear owner and exit path. Bound fan-out, pass context cancellation, inspect goroutine counts and profiles, and use the race detector; replace goroutines with a queue or worker service when work must survive process failure.', 'A network service runs one bounded worker per active task and cancels the group when the request deadline expires, preventing leaked background work.', 'Starting is cheap; lifetime and ownership are not.'),
    lesson('Channels transfer values and synchronization between goroutines. Use them to communicate ownership or coordinate pipelines; do not force every shared counter through a channel when a mutex is clearer.', 'Define who closes the channel, bound capacity from throughput and memory, select on cancellation, and avoid sends with no receiver. Test shutdown, slow consumers, and race conditions.', 'A log processor uses a bounded job channel, fixed workers, and context cancellation so overload becomes visible instead of consuming unbounded memory.', 'The sender sends; the owner closes; capacity is backpressure policy.'),
    lesson('Go interfaces describe behavior implicitly. Keep them small and usually define them where they are consumed; accept interfaces and return concrete types unless abstraction has a real caller.', 'Avoid speculative interfaces and typed-nil traps. Use compile-time conformance checks sparingly and test behavior with small fakes rather than mocking every method.', 'A service defines a two-method Clock interface only because deterministic time is needed in tests; its repository returns concrete domain values.', 'Interfaces belong to consumers, not implementers.'),
    lesson('context carries cancellation, deadlines, and request-scoped metadata across call boundaries. It is not a bag for optional parameters or long-lived application state.', 'Pass context as the first argument, never store it in a struct, call cancel functions, and ensure blocking work selects or checks cancellation. Trace deadline causes and goroutine growth.', 'When a client disconnects, the HTTP context cancels database and downstream calls so the service stops spending capacity on an abandoned response.', 'Context answers: is this work still wanted?'),
    lesson('Go tests favor observable behavior, table cases, small fakes, and the standard testing package. Add integration tests where real database or network semantics matter and fuzz parsers with strong invariants.', 'Run tests with -race, avoid time.Sleep synchronization, control time through dependencies, test cancellation and shutdown, and benchmark only representative hot paths.', 'A protocol parser uses table tests and fuzzing, while a service shutdown test waits on explicit signals rather than hoping a sleep is long enough.', 'Signal, do not sleep; test failure as a first-class path.'),
  ],
  'web-fundamentals': [
    lesson('Semantic HTML gives content native meaning, keyboard behavior, form semantics, and an accessibility tree before JavaScript runs. Choose elements for purpose, then style them.', 'Use headings in order, labels for controls, buttons for actions, links for navigation, and landmarks for page regions. Verify with keyboard-only use and the browser accessibility tree.', 'A commerce product selector uses fieldset, legend, labels, and buttons, so it works with screen readers and without custom keyboard code.', 'Use the element whose built-in contract matches the job.'),
    lesson('CSS layout uses normal flow, flexbox for one-dimensional alignment, grid for two-dimensional placement, and intrinsic sizing to negotiate available space. Responsive design should follow content breakpoints rather than device names.', 'Prevent overflow with min-width: 0 on flexible children, use logical properties, test zoom and long content, and avoid fixed heights for text regions.', 'A product grid uses auto-fit and minmax so cards reflow naturally from small phones to ultrawide screens without JavaScript width checks.', 'Let content size first; constrain only where necessary.'),
    lesson('Accessibility means people can perceive, operate, understand, and reliably use the interface with diverse input and output methods. WCAG is a testable baseline, not a substitute for user testing.', 'Preserve focus order, visible focus, contrast, names, error associations, reduced motion, and touch target size. Test keyboard, screen reader smoke flows, zoom, and automated rules.', 'Checkout announces validation errors, moves focus only when necessary, and remains usable at 200% zoom with a keyboard.', 'Name it, focus it, announce it, and keep it operable.'),
    lesson('A browser resolves DNS, establishes transport and TLS, sends HTTP, parses streamed bytes, builds DOM and CSSOM, lays out, paints, and composites. Caching and connection reuse can matter more than JavaScript micro-optimizations.', 'Use DevTools waterfalls and server timing to locate DNS, connection, TTFB, transfer, or main-thread delays. Validate cache headers, compression, priorities, and third-party cost.', 'A slow storefront is fixed by caching immutable assets and reducing origin TTFB after the network waterfall shows JavaScript execution was not the bottleneck.', 'Follow the request from name lookup to pixels.'),
    lesson('Core Web Vitals measure user experience: LCP for loading, INP for interaction responsiveness, and CLS for visual stability. Field data matters because lab hardware and networks are only controlled samples.', 'Attribute LCP to its resource and server path, INP to event and rendering work, and CLS to unsized or late content. Segment real-user data by page and device, then set regression budgets.', 'A commerce page reserves image and promotion space, preloads only its true hero, and breaks a long click handler into smaller work, improving CLS, LCP, and INP.', 'Load the main thing, respond quickly, move nothing unexpectedly.'),
  ],
  react: [
    lesson('React renders a description of UI from props and state, then commits changes. Rendering must stay pure because React may render, pause, discard, or repeat work before a commit.', 'Keep side effects out of render, use stable keys for identity, and measure commits with the profiler. Do not add memoization until a measured re-render is expensive.', 'An operations dashboard derives filtered rows during render and uses row IDs as keys, so sorting does not move local row state to another record.', 'Render describes; commit changes; effects synchronize.'),
    lesson('State should live at the lowest common owner that needs to change it. Store minimal source-of-truth data and derive the rest; use server-state tools for remote caches rather than duplicating them in component state.', 'Model transitions explicitly, avoid mirrored props and deeply global state, and separate URL, server, form, and ephemeral UI state by lifetime.', 'A dashboard keeps filters in the URL, fetched incidents in a query cache, and the open popover locally instead of putting all three in one global store.', 'Place state by owner and lifetime.'),
    lesson('An effect synchronizes React with an external system such as a subscription, timer, network API, or imperative widget. If a value can be calculated during render or handled in an event, no effect is needed.', 'Declare every reactive dependency, clean up subscriptions, abort stale work, and make setup safe to repeat. Avoid effect chains that encode business transitions indirectly.', 'A live incident view subscribes by incident ID and unsubscribes on change; sorting the incident list remains a pure calculation with no effect.', 'No external system, usually no effect.'),
    lesson('Accessible React begins with semantic HTML and predictable focus, then adds ARIA only when native behavior cannot express the widget. Component reuse must preserve labels and keyboard behavior.', 'Generate stable IDs, forward accessible names, restore focus after dialogs, respect reduced motion, and test composed components—not just primitives.', 'A shared dialog traps focus while open, labels itself from its title, closes with Escape, and returns focus to the button that opened it.', 'Composition must preserve the browser’s accessibility contract.'),
    lesson('React performance work starts by locating user-visible delay. Reduce unnecessary work, component breadth, bundle cost, and expensive rendering before reaching for blanket memoization.', 'Profile slow interactions, virtualize genuinely large lists, transition non-urgent updates, keep state local, and verify gains with production-like data and INP.', 'A dashboard improves filtering by moving state closer to the table and virtualizing 20,000 rows; memoizing every button had shown no measurable benefit.', 'Measure interaction, find work, remove or defer it.'),
  ],
  nextjs: [
    lesson('Server Components render on the server and can access server-only data without shipping their component code to the browser. Client Components are required for state, effects, event handlers, and browser APIs.', 'Place the client boundary as low as practical, pass serializable props, and never import secrets into client code. Inspect bundle output rather than assuming a file is server-only.', 'A SaaS settings page fetches account data in a Server Component and isolates one interactive theme control as a small Client Component.', 'Server by default; client at the interaction boundary.'),
    lesson('The App Router maps folders to layouts, pages, loading states, errors, route groups, and dynamic segments. Layouts preserve shared UI while navigation streams route segments.', 'Keep route files thin, validate dynamic parameters, use notFound and error boundaries intentionally, and understand that search parameters and cookies make routes dynamic.', 'A SaaS separates public and protected route groups while sharing one root theme layout and colocated loading/error states.', 'Folders define URL structure; route groups define organization without URL cost.'),
    lesson('Next.js caching has separate layers for request memoization, data, full routes, and the client router. Correctness starts by stating how fresh data must be and which mutation invalidates it.', 'Use explicit cache modes, revalidation, tags, and dynamic APIs deliberately. Never cache personalized or authorization-dependent data publicly, and test stale and invalidation paths.', 'A public article list revalidates by tag after publishing, while account billing reads dynamically and sends private no-store responses.', 'Cache only data whose staleness and audience are explicit.'),
    lesson('Route Handlers expose HTTP endpoints inside the App Router. They should validate input, authenticate independently, call application services, and return stable status and cache semantics.', 'Keep credentials server-only, enforce authorization on every request, avoid trusting tenant IDs from clients, and make mutation origin/idempotency rules explicit.', 'An answer endpoint validates the track and question ID, resolves the session, calls a library service, and returns private no-store JSON.', 'HTTP boundary validates; service decides; provider fetches.'),
    lesson('Next.js performance combines server response time, cache correctness, streamed rendering, client bundle size, image/font delivery, and interaction cost. Faster static output is not useful if it leaks or serves stale private data.', 'Measure route timing, RSC payload, JavaScript bundles, Web Vitals, and cache hit behavior. Optimize the dominant delay and keep a rollback for caching changes.', 'A content SaaS streams the page shell, caches public catalog data, keeps user data dynamic, and removes a large client-only dependency from the initial route.', 'Send less, stream sooner, cache safely, measure users.'),
  ],
  angular: [
    lesson('Angular components combine a template, state, dependency boundary, and lifecycle. Keep presentation focused, use inputs and outputs for clear contracts, and move domain operations to services.', 'Prefer predictable data flow, track repeated items by stable identity, clean up external resources, and test rendered behavior rather than private methods.', 'An admin table component receives rows and emits selection while a service owns loading and authorization-aware operations.', 'Components present; services coordinate.'),
    lesson('Angular dependency injection resolves providers from hierarchical injectors. Scope determines sharing and lifetime, so root, route, and component providers can produce very different state behavior.', 'Use injection tokens for configuration, avoid service-locator patterns, and choose scope deliberately. Diagnose duplicated state by inspecting where the provider is declared.', 'A wizard provides draft state at the route boundary so each wizard instance is isolated while the API client remains a root singleton.', 'Provider location defines instance lifetime.'),
    lesson('RxJS Observables represent values over time with cancellation and composition. Operator choice defines concurrency: switchMap replaces, concatMap queues, mergeMap overlaps, and exhaustMap ignores while busy.', 'Handle teardown, errors, retry limits, multicasting, and backpressure expectations. Avoid nested subscriptions; model the stream with operators and test with controlled time.', 'Type-ahead search uses debounce plus switchMap so a new query cancels the stale HTTP request and only the newest result updates the view.', 'Choose flattening by the business concurrency rule.'),
    lesson('Reactive forms model controls explicitly and suit complex validation and dynamic structure; template forms suit simpler screens. Client validation improves feedback but never replaces server validation.', 'Keep cross-field rules at the right group, show errors accessibly, prevent duplicate submission, and map server errors back to controls without losing user input.', 'An enterprise onboarding form validates date ranges and asynchronously checks an identifier, while the API repeats every security-sensitive rule.', 'Validate near the user and again at the trust boundary.'),
    lesson('Angular change detection updates bindings when scheduled work or reactive signals indicate change. OnPush and signals reduce unnecessary checking only when state updates remain explicit and immutable enough to observe.', 'Profile before tuning, avoid mutating inputs invisibly, use trackBy, and keep expensive calculations out of templates. Verify interaction latency with realistic lists.', 'An administration grid uses OnPush, immutable row updates, and stable tracking, reducing checks without manual detectChanges calls scattered across components.', 'Make change visible; let the framework schedule rendering.'),
  ],
  nodejs: [
    lesson('Node.js runs JavaScript on an event loop while delegating many I/O operations to the operating system or worker pool. It excels at concurrent I/O but one long synchronous task can delay every request.', 'Measure event-loop delay, CPU, worker-pool saturation, and memory. Stream or offload heavy work, bound concurrency, and never confuse many connections with unlimited capacity.', 'An API gateway handles thousands of sockets but sends PDF rendering to worker processes so request routing remains responsive.', 'One loop coordinates; blocking one task delays all.'),
    lesson('Node streams process data incrementally and propagate backpressure when producers outpace consumers. pipe or pipeline handles flow and error propagation more safely than manually wiring data events.', 'Respect write return values, bound transforms, handle abort and errors, and avoid buffering entire uploads. Test slow consumers and partial failures.', 'A gateway streams a multi-gigabyte export from object storage to the client with pipeline instead of holding it in heap memory.', 'Chunks plus backpressure keep memory bounded.'),
    lesson('Good APIs expose stable resources and operations, validate at the boundary, use meaningful status codes, and make pagination, idempotency, versioning, and errors explicit.', 'Authorize every resource independently, cap request sizes and rates, document retry safety, and add correlation IDs. Compatibility matters more than internal elegance.', 'A payment creation endpoint requires an idempotency key and returns the same result after a client timeout and retry.', 'Define contract, trust boundary, retry behavior, and evolution.'),
    lesson('Node security depends on treating all inputs and dependencies as untrusted. Validate payloads, parameterize queries, encode output by context, protect secrets, and keep authorization server-side.', 'Set secure headers and cookies, limit parsers and uploads, scan dependencies, prevent SSRF with destination policy, and log decisions without sensitive data.', 'A webhook endpoint verifies the raw-body signature before parsing, rejects replayed timestamps, and never logs the shared secret.', 'Validate shape, verify identity, authorize action, limit impact.'),
    lesson('Observability connects logs, metrics, and traces through request context. Record latency, traffic, errors, and saturation at service and dependency boundaries, not every internal line.', 'Use structured fields, redact secrets and personal data, sample traces deliberately, and alert on user-impacting symptoms with runbooks.', 'A gateway trace shows latency is in one partner API while event-loop delay remains healthy, preventing an unnecessary Node rewrite.', 'Observe the request path and the constrained resource.'),
  ],
  'spring-boot': [
    lesson('Spring dependency injection builds an application graph from beans. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit, supports immutability, and exposes cycles early.', 'Keep bean scopes deliberate, avoid field injection and service-locator access, and separate configuration from domain rules. A cycle usually signals confused ownership.', 'An order service injects repository and payment ports through its constructor, so unit tests use small fakes without starting Spring.', 'Dependencies are required inputs, not hidden globals.'),
    lesson('A Spring REST API maps HTTP contracts to validated request models and application operations. Controllers should translate transport concerns, not own transactions or business policy.', 'Use consistent errors, validation, pagination, idempotency, content types, and authorization. Never expose persistence entities as an accidental public contract.', 'An order controller validates the command, calls one application service, and maps a duplicate idempotency key to the documented response.', 'Controller translates; application service decides.'),
    lesson('A transaction defines one atomic database boundary, not a distributed guarantee. Spring proxy-based @Transactional behavior depends on call path, propagation, isolation, and exception handling.', 'Keep transactions short, avoid remote calls inside them, understand rollback rules, test locking on the real database, and use an outbox for reliable post-commit events.', 'Order creation writes the order and outbox event together; payment happens asynchronously rather than holding a database transaction across HTTP.', 'One database transaction cannot atomically own another service.'),
    lesson('Spring Security separates authentication from authorization through filter chains and a security context. Method and resource checks still need explicit ownership and tenant rules.', 'Use modern password hashing, short-lived tokens or secure sessions, CSRF protection where cookies authenticate, least privilege, and deny-by-default route rules.', 'A user with a valid token can read only orders belonging to the active organization, enforced again in the service query.', 'Valid identity does not imply permission to this resource.'),
    lesson('Spring Boot observability uses structured logging, Micrometer metrics, traces, health indicators, and actuator endpoints. Health should describe whether traffic can be served, not expose internals publicly.', 'Measure request and dependency latency, pool saturation, JVM/GC, errors, and queue depth. Protect actuator data and preserve trace context across async boundaries.', 'An order latency alert is traced to database pool exhaustion, confirmed by pool wait metrics before any scaling change.', 'Observe user symptom, dependency time, and saturated resource.'),
  ],
  'aspnet-core': [
    lesson('ASP.NET Core middleware forms an ordered request pipeline. Each component may act before and after the next delegate or short-circuit entirely, so order changes security and behavior.', 'Place exception handling, forwarding, authentication, authorization, routing, and endpoints deliberately. Keep middleware stateless unless its lifetime is safe.', 'A tenant context middleware runs after authentication but before authorization so policies evaluate the verified tenant rather than an untrusted header.', 'Pipeline order is part of the security model.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET Core DI supports transient, scoped, and singleton lifetimes. Scoped commonly means one request; injecting a scoped service into a singleton creates invalid lifetime ownership.', 'Use constructor injection, keep singletons thread-safe, avoid service-location, and create explicit scopes only for owned background work.', 'A background queue creates a scope per job to obtain DbContext instead of capturing a request-scoped context forever.', 'The longer-lived object must not own a shorter-lived dependency.'),
    lesson('A Web API should bind and validate transport models, authenticate and authorize, invoke application logic, and produce a stable HTTP contract. Minimal APIs and controllers share these responsibilities.', 'Use problem details, idempotency, pagination, cancellation, request limits, and explicit versioning. Keep domain and EF entities out of public responses.', 'A tenant invoice endpoint resolves tenant from the session, not the route payload, and passes RequestAborted into the service.', 'Bind, validate, authorize, execute, translate.'),
    lesson('EF Core tracks entities and translates LINQ to provider queries. Correctness and performance depend on query shape, transaction boundaries, concurrency handling, and the actual database provider.', 'Project required columns, avoid N+1 access, inspect generated SQL, use no-tracking for reads, and handle optimistic concurrency explicitly. Do not share DbContext across threads.', 'A billing list projects directly to DTOs and uses a row-version token to reject conflicting edits instead of silently overwriting them.', 'Query translation and unit-of-work lifetime are the real abstraction.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET authentication establishes a principal; authorization policies decide whether that principal may perform the action. Resource ownership and tenant membership must be checked using trusted server data.', 'Validate issuer, audience, lifetime, cookie properties, CSRF behavior, and key rotation. Prefer policy handlers for reusable resource rules and log denials without tokens.', 'A valid business user receives 404 for another tenant’s document because the repository query is organization-scoped and the policy requires membership.', 'Identity first; policy and resource scope second.'),
  ],
  'django-fastapi': [
    lesson('Django forms/serializers and FastAPI models turn untrusted request data into validated types. Validation should cover shape and local invariants; business and authorization rules still belong in services.', 'Reject unknown or oversized inputs where appropriate, normalize deliberately, return stable field errors, and never trust client-supplied ownership fields.', 'An inference API validates model name and input size, then the service resolves the allowed tenant model from the authenticated account.', 'Parsing makes data shaped; authorization makes the action allowed.'),
    lesson('Django ORM and SQLAlchemy-style data access translate object operations into SQL. Convenience does not remove joins, indexes, transactions, connection limits, or N+1 behavior.', 'Inspect queries, select related data deliberately, paginate, keep transactions short, and test against the production database engine.', 'A customer portal removes hundreds of repeated queries with select_related and an index confirmed by EXPLAIN.', 'Objects are convenient; the database still executes SQL.'),
    lesson('Authentication proves identity; application authorization decides permissions and object ownership. Framework defaults help, but every resource query must preserve tenant and ownership scope.', 'Use secure cookies or validated tokens, CSRF protection for cookie-authenticated mutations, modern password hashing, rate limits, and consistent denial behavior.', 'A customer can retrieve an invoice only through a query filtered by both invoice ID and authenticated organization ID.', 'Never fetch broadly and authorize afterward when a scoped query can do both.'),
    lesson('Async Python helps when the entire path uses nonblocking I/O. Calling synchronous ORM, CPU work, or blocking libraries inside an async endpoint stalls the event loop.', 'Choose a consistent boundary, use thread adapters only knowingly, bound concurrency, propagate cancellation, and move durable or CPU-heavy work to workers.', 'FastAPI handles concurrent model-service calls asynchronously, while Django background exports run in a job worker and persist status.', 'Async is only as nonblocking as the slowest call path.'),
    lesson('Framework tests should cover plain business rules, request validation and authorization, database behavior, and a small number of full journeys. Select the cheapest layer that proves the risk.', 'Use real database transactions for ORM behavior, override dependencies explicitly, test async cancellation and permission failures, and avoid snapshots that hide incorrect details.', 'A portal has unit tests for invoice rules, API tests for tenant isolation, and one browser payment flow.', 'Test rule, boundary, persistence, then journey.'),
  ],
  'sql-postgresql': [
    lesson('Relational modeling stores facts once where practical and uses keys, constraints, and relationships to preserve meaning. Normalize to protect correctness, then denormalize only for a measured read pattern with an update strategy.', 'Use primary, foreign, unique, check, and not-null constraints as the final integrity boundary. Review cardinality, lifecycle, tenancy, and audit requirements before choosing tables.', 'A subscription platform stores invoices and line items relationally, with immutable price snapshots and constraints preventing duplicate provider references.', 'Model facts, identity, relationships, and invariants.'),
    lesson('A PostgreSQL index is an ordered access structure that can avoid scanning or sorting, but it costs storage and write work. Column order, predicates, selectivity, and query shape decide whether it helps.', 'Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE with real distributions, match composite indexes to equality/range/order needs, remove redundant indexes, and monitor bloat plus write overhead.', 'An unpaid-invoice query uses a partial composite index on organization and due date, shrinking both query time and index size.', 'Index the query pattern, not every column.'),
    lesson('A transaction groups changes atomically and isolation defines which concurrent effects may be observed. PostgreSQL MVCC lets readers and writers coexist but does not eliminate lost updates or business races.', 'Keep transactions short, lock only required rows, order locks consistently, retry serialization failures, and enforce invariants with constraints where possible.', 'Two payment callbacks race, but a unique provider-event constraint and transactional state change make processing idempotent.', 'Atomicity groups changes; isolation controls interference.'),
    lesson('A query plan is PostgreSQL’s chosen execution strategy based on statistics and estimated costs. A slow query may result from bad cardinality estimates, missing access paths, spills, lock waits, or simply returning too much data.', 'Compare estimated and actual rows, inspect buffers, sorts, loops, and timing, refresh statistics, and verify parameter behavior. Do not force an index before understanding the plan.', 'An invoice report is slow because a nested loop repeats an underestimated lookup; extended statistics and a query rewrite fix the estimate and plan.', 'Read plan nodes as data flow: rows, loops, buffers, time.'),
    lesson('PostgreSQL concurrency combines MVCC snapshots, row and table locks, deadlock detection, and isolation levels. Blocking is often correct; unbounded blocking without visibility is not.', 'Set sensible timeouts, inspect pg_stat_activity and lock graphs, keep transaction scope small, use consistent lock order, and test contention with production-like concurrency.', 'Billing workers claim small batches with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, process independently, and expose queue age so throughput and starvation are visible.', 'Know who waits for which lock and for how long.'),
  ],
  'mongodb-nosql': [
    lesson('MongoDB document modeling groups data that is read and changed together. Embed bounded owned data; reference independently growing or shared data, because documents have a 16 MB limit and unbounded arrays become operational debt.', 'Design from access patterns, atomicity needs, cardinality, growth, and lifecycle. Add schema validation even when fields evolve.', 'A catalog embeds a bounded set of product attributes but stores high-volume reviews separately with product references and pagination.', 'Embed togetherness; reference independent growth.'),
    lesson('MongoDB indexes support query predicates and sort order but increase write cost and memory pressure. Compound order should reflect equality, sort, then range patterns rather than field popularity.', 'Use explain executionStats, inspect keys versus documents examined, create unique or partial indexes for invariants, and remove redundant indexes after workload evidence.', 'A catalog query uses a compound category, availability, and price index that both filters and sorts without an in-memory sort.', 'Index the full access pattern and verify scan efficiency.'),
    lesson('An aggregation pipeline transforms documents stage by stage. Put selective matches early, project only needed fields, and understand when sorting, grouping, lookup, or unwind expands memory and cardinality.', 'Inspect the explain plan, indexes, stage output sizes, spill behavior, and limits. Precompute stable expensive summaries when freshness permits.', 'A sales dashboard filters tenant and date using an index before grouping, instead of unwinding the entire global order collection.', 'Reduce rows early; expand and group only what remains.'),
    lesson('MongoDB consistency depends on replica-set behavior, read concern, write concern, sessions, and whether an operation spans documents. Single-document writes are atomic; multi-document transactions add coordination and cost.', 'Choose concerns from acceptable data loss and staleness, use retryable idempotent operations, test primary failover, and avoid reading from lagging replicas for read-after-write requirements.', 'A catalog tolerates slightly stale search reads, while inventory reservation uses majority writes and a guarded atomic update on one stock document.', 'State the stale-read and acknowledged-write contract.'),
    lesson('MongoDB scales through replicas for availability and reads, and sharding for data distribution. A shard key must distribute writes while supporting routing; an ever-increasing hot key can bottleneck one shard.', 'Measure working set, connections, replication lag, chunk balance, document growth, and scatter-gather queries. Sharding does not repair a poor schema or missing indexes.', 'A global catalog shards by a hashed tenant-aware key while keeping tenant-scoped queries targeted and avoiding one hot ingestion partition.', 'Distribute load without destroying query locality.'),
  ],
  'redis-caching': [
    lesson('Cache-aside loads missing data from the source and writes it to cache; write-through updates cache with the write; write-behind delays persistence and risks loss. Select a pattern from consistency and failure requirements.', 'Define key ownership, TTL, invalidation, stampede protection, and fallback. Treat the database as truth unless the design explicitly makes Redis authoritative.', 'A read-heavy product API uses cache-aside with jittered TTL and request coalescing so one expiry does not create thousands of database reads.', 'Every cache needs truth, expiry, invalidation, and failure behavior.'),
    lesson('Expiration limits staleness and memory, but TTL is not synchronization. Fixed simultaneous expiry can create a stampede; no expiry can preserve incorrect data indefinitely.', 'Add jitter, proactive refresh for hot keys, explicit invalidation for critical writes, and metrics for hit rate, age, eviction, and fallback latency.', 'Product summaries expire around five minutes with random jitter, while price changes publish immediate invalidations.', 'TTL bounds age; invalidation follows important change.'),
    lesson('Redis strings, hashes, sets, sorted sets, streams, and other structures provide different atomic operations. Choose the smallest structure matching access and concurrency needs, not the most feature-rich one.', 'Bound cardinality, estimate memory overhead, use atomic server operations or scripts for invariants, and avoid dangerous full-keyspace commands.', 'A leaderboard uses a sorted set for rank updates and range reads instead of reading every score into application memory.', 'Choose by operation: lookup, membership, rank, queue, or stream.'),
    lesson('A cache creates a second copy of data, so perfect consistency is rarely free. Define whether stale reads, missed invalidations, duplicate work, or temporary source reads are acceptable.', 'Use versioned keys or events where needed, make writes idempotent, prevent stale repopulation races, and test reorder, delay, and cache loss.', 'A price update increments a version used in the cache key, preventing an older in-flight request from overwriting the newly cached value.', 'Name the tolerated stale window and the repair path.'),
    lesson('Redis is an optional accelerator unless explicitly designed as a durable system. Applications must handle timeout, failover, eviction, restart, and overload without turning a cache incident into a total outage.', 'Set short timeouts, cap retries, use circuit breaking, protect the primary database from fallback storms, and monitor memory, evictions, latency, replication, and connection saturation.', 'When Redis fails, an API serves a bounded subset from PostgreSQL under tighter rate limits instead of retrying every cache request indefinitely.', 'Fail open or closed deliberately, and protect the system of record.'),
  ],
  'linux-networking': [
    lesson('A Linux process owns an address space and resources; threads share that process state. The scheduler, signals, file descriptors, cgroups, and namespaces shape what the process can do.', 'Inspect process state, CPU, memory, open files, syscalls, and cgroup limits before restarting. A high load average can mean runnable or uninterruptible tasks, not only CPU use.', 'A service latency spike is traced to processes waiting on blocked disk I/O rather than a shortage of CPU cores.', 'PID identifies; state explains; limits constrain.'),
    lesson('Unix permissions combine owner, group, mode bits, ACLs, and directory traversal rights. A user needs execute permission on every parent directory to reach a file.', 'Use least privilege, dedicated service users, safe umask, and explicit ownership. Diagnose with namei, stat, id, getfacl, and audit logs instead of chmod 777.', 'A deployment fixes a read failure by granting the service group directory traversal and file read access, not world write access.', 'Directory execute means traverse; file execute means run.'),
    lesson('TCP provides an ordered byte stream with connection state, retransmission, flow control, and congestion control; DNS maps names to records with caching and TTL. Application messages still need their own framing and timeout rules.', 'Trace name resolution, address family, handshake, TLS, retransmits, socket state, and return path. Use dig, ss, tcpdump, and connection metrics.', 'Intermittent API failures are caused by stale DNS in a long-lived client pool; TTL-aware refresh fixes the actual path rather than increasing retries.', 'Resolve name, connect transport, negotiate TLS, exchange framed application data.'),
    lesson('Resource analysis uses saturation and contention, not just average utilization. CPU run queue, memory pressure, swapping, disk latency, file descriptors, sockets, and cgroup throttling can each cap a service.', 'Correlate top-level symptoms with per-process and kernel evidence using ps, top, vmstat, iostat, pidstat, ss, and pressure stall information.', 'A container shows low average CPU but high throttled time because its quota is exhausted during bursts; adjusting work and limits removes tail latency.', 'Utilization says busy; saturation says work is waiting.'),
    lesson('Production troubleshooting should preserve evidence and reduce the search space: establish impact and timeline, compare healthy instances, follow dependencies, form one hypothesis, then run the safest discriminating test.', 'Capture logs, metrics, traces, process state, network evidence, and recent change history. Mitigate safely, document the cause, and add detection plus prevention.', 'A connection incident is mitigated by draining one bad node, then proven to be ephemeral-port exhaustion from missing client reuse.', 'Scope, compare, hypothesize, test, mitigate, prevent.'),
  ],
  docker: [
    lesson('A Docker image is an immutable content-addressed set of layers plus metadata; a container adds a writable layer and runtime isolation. Image layers are shared, so build order affects cache reuse and size.', 'Pin base images, copy dependency manifests before source, use multi-stage builds, inspect image history, and rebuild for security fixes rather than patching running containers.', 'A service compiles in a builder stage and copies only the binary and certificates into a small non-root runtime image.', 'Image is the recipe result; container is one running instance.'),
    lesson('Docker networking gives containers isolated interfaces, DNS-based service discovery on user-defined networks, port publishing, and host or overlay connectivity. localhost inside a container refers to that container.', 'Inspect names, routes, published ports, listeners, DNS, and host firewalls. Bind services deliberately and avoid exposing internal databases to every host interface.', 'An API connects to postgres:5432 by service name on a private network while only the API port is published to the host.', 'Container localhost is not host localhost.'),
    lesson('Container writable files disappear with replacement. Use volumes for managed local persistence, bind mounts for explicit host paths, and object or database services for durable shared data.', 'Define ownership, backup, restore, portability, and concurrent-access semantics. Never assume an image rebuild preserves runtime data.', 'Local PostgreSQL uses a named volume with tested backups; uploaded documents go to object storage so replicas can be replaced freely.', 'Container filesystem is disposable; durable state needs an owner.'),
    lesson('Container security reduces privileges and attack surface but is not a magical sandbox. Run as non-root, drop capabilities, use read-only filesystems where possible, scan images, and keep secrets outside layers.', 'Pin trusted bases, patch through rebuilds, use seccomp/AppArmor, limit resources, and never mount the Docker socket into an untrusted workload.', 'A web service runs with a numeric non-root UID, no extra capabilities, a read-only root filesystem, and a writable temporary mount.', 'Small image, small privilege, small writable surface.'),
    lesson('Fast Docker builds depend on cache-friendly layers, a small build context, deterministic dependencies, and multi-stage outputs. Smaller images improve transfer and attack surface but should not sacrifice debuggability blindly.', 'Use .dockerignore, BuildKit cache mounts and secret mounts, pinned dependencies, and CI checks for size and vulnerabilities. Verify the final stage contains no build secrets.', 'A Node build caches npm artifacts, copies lockfiles first, and excludes tests and local modules from the build context.', 'Stable inputs first; changing source later; runtime gets only outputs.'),
  ],
  kubernetes: [
    lesson('A Pod is the smallest scheduled unit and shares network and volumes among its containers. Deployments manage replaceable stateless replicas; StatefulSets add stable identity and ordered storage semantics; Jobs represent completion.', 'Set requests and limits from evidence, use probes for correct purposes, handle termination, and choose a controller matching lifecycle. A pod is disposable even when its workload matters.', 'A stateless API uses a Deployment, while a one-time migration uses a Job with explicit retry and timeout behavior.', 'Choose controller by lifecycle: serve, retain identity, or finish.'),
    lesson('A Service gives stable virtual discovery over changing pods; ingress or Gateway API routes external HTTP traffic. Readiness decides which pods receive traffic, while network policies constrain allowed paths.', 'Trace DNS, service selectors, endpoints, readiness, ingress rules, TLS, and policies. Avoid debugging the application before proving the network path.', 'A rollout sends traffic only to ready API pods through a ClusterIP Service and an HTTPS gateway with managed certificates.', 'Name resolves to Service; selector finds ready endpoints; policy permits the path.'),
    lesson('ConfigMaps hold non-secret configuration and Secrets hold sensitive values in the Kubernetes API, but base64 is not encryption. Workloads need a deliberate update and rotation strategy.', 'Use external secret integration where appropriate, encrypt etcd, restrict RBAC, avoid environment leaks, checksum mounted config for rollouts, and test rotation.', 'A platform mounts short-lived database credentials from an external secret provider and rolls pods when non-secret feature configuration changes.', 'Separate config from secret; plan propagation and rotation.'),
    lesson('Kubernetes security spans identity, RBAC, admission, pod security, images, network, nodes, and secrets. Namespaces organize scope but do not create full isolation by themselves.', 'Use least-privilege service accounts, restricted pod security, signed/scanned images, network policies, resource quotas, and audited admin access.', 'A tenant worker cannot call the Kubernetes API, runs without root or capabilities, and can connect only to its queue and database.', 'Secure identity, workload, network, supply chain, and control plane.'),
    lesson('Reliable Kubernetes workloads need correct probes, disruption budgets, topology spread, autoscaling signals, graceful shutdown, capacity headroom, and tested failure recovery.', 'Measure availability, latency, errors, saturation, restart causes, pending pods, and rollout status. Test node loss and dependency failure rather than assuming replication is resilience.', 'A service keeps replicas across zones, drains in-flight work on SIGTERM, and blocks rollout progress when error rate rises.', 'Replicas help only when failure domains and shutdown behavior are correct.'),
  ],
  'cicd-infrastructure': [
    lesson('A delivery pipeline turns one reviewed commit into a tested, traceable artifact promoted through environments. Build once and promote the same artifact to avoid environment-specific drift.', 'Order fast deterministic checks before expensive ones, isolate untrusted contributions, retain provenance, and make failures actionable. Do not place production secrets in build jobs that do not need them.', 'Several services build signed images once, run unit and integration checks, then promote digests through staging and production approvals.', 'Commit to artifact to evidence to promotion.'),
    lesson('Terraform declares desired infrastructure and compares it with state and provider reality. State is sensitive coordination data; modules should represent stable reusable boundaries, not hide every resource.', 'Use remote locked state, reviewed plans, pinned providers, import existing resources, and small blast-radius states. Never edit state casually or apply an unreviewed surprise plan.', 'A team separates shared networking state from each service, so one application change cannot accidentally replace the production VPC.', 'Configuration declares; state remembers; plan explains; apply changes.'),
    lesson('CI/CD secrets should be short-lived, narrowly scoped, auditable, and injected only into the job that needs them. Federated workload identity is safer than long-lived cloud keys.', 'Masking is not protection if a tool can exfiltrate a secret. Restrict fork workflows, rotate credentials, pin actions, and prevent secrets from entering images, caches, logs, or artifacts.', 'A deployment job exchanges its verified repository identity for a short-lived cloud token instead of storing an administrator key in CI.', 'Prefer identity exchange over stored credentials.'),
    lesson('Rolling, blue-green, and canary deployments trade capacity, speed, rollback, and exposure. Database and event changes must remain compatible across old and new versions during transition.', 'Define automated health gates from user metrics, bound canary exposure, preserve artifact rollback, and use expand-migrate-contract for schemas.', 'A canary receives 5% of traffic and advances only when latency and error budgets remain healthy; an incompatible schema cleanup waits until all old code is gone.', 'Deploy code gradually; evolve data compatibly.'),
    lesson('Delivery observability connects a commit and artifact to rollout events and service outcomes. A green pipeline proves checks passed, not that users are healthy.', 'Track deployment frequency, lead time, failure rate, recovery time, rollout duration, and post-deploy SLO signals. Record who promoted what and provide one-command rollback or roll-forward guidance.', 'When errors rise, the team links traces to the new image digest, pauses promotion, and rolls back while retaining evidence for diagnosis.', 'Trace commit to artifact to deployment to user impact.'),
  ],
  'system-design': [
    lesson('System design begins by separating functional behavior from measurable quality constraints: users, operations, data, scale, latency, availability, durability, security, compliance, and cost.', 'Quantify normal and peak load, data size, growth, and recovery objectives. State assumptions and identify the few constraints that actually shape architecture.', 'A notification platform first defines delivery channels, peak fan-out, latency SLO, duplicate tolerance, and regional failure objectives before drawing services.', 'Requirements first; numbers reveal the architecture.'),
    lesson('APIs and service boundaries should follow cohesive capabilities and ownership, not individual database tables. Contracts need idempotency, pagination, versioning, authentication, authorization, and failure semantics.', 'Prefer a modular monolith until independent scaling, failure isolation, ownership, or deployment cadence justifies distribution. Keep internal data private to its owner.', 'A notification capability owns preferences and delivery orchestration behind one API rather than creating a service for every endpoint.', 'Boundary by capability; distribute only for a measurable reason.'),
    lesson('Data architecture chooses sources of truth, models, indexes, replication, partitioning, retention, and event flows from access and consistency requirements. Every duplicate copy needs an update and repair story.', 'Document transactional boundaries, idempotency, ordering, backfills, privacy deletion, backups, and restore tests. Prefer constraints over application hope.', 'A notification request is durable in one relational source, while delivery workers consume an outbox event and build a repairable read model.', 'For each datum: owner, readers, consistency, retention, recovery.'),
    lesson('Scaling means finding and relieving the first constrained resource while preserving correctness. Stateless replicas help compute, partitioning helps distribution, caching helps repeated reads, and queues absorb temporary rate mismatch.', 'Use load estimates, capacity tests, saturation metrics, backpressure, admission control, and a degradation plan. Avoid adding distributed machinery before proving the bottleneck.', 'A global activity feed starts with read replicas and cache, then partitions only after measured write concentration exceeds the primary’s safe headroom.', 'Measure the bottleneck; scale that resource; preserve backpressure.'),
    lesson('Every architecture trades consistency, latency, availability, complexity, cost, and operability. A strong design names the rejected alternatives and the threshold that would change the decision.', 'Run failure-mode reviews and threat models, define SLOs and budgets, estimate cost drivers, test recovery, and keep migration paths reversible where uncertainty is high.', 'A platform chooses at-least-once delivery with idempotent consumers over expensive global exactly-once claims and documents the duplicate-rate objective.', 'Decision, trade-off, failure, proof, change threshold.'),
  ],
  'distributed-systems': [
    lesson('Distributed consistency describes what values operations may observe across replicas and time. Stronger guarantees simplify reasoning but add coordination latency and reduce availability during partitions.', 'State invariants and read-after-write needs per operation, not for the whole product. Use versioning, quorum, or transactions where needed and tolerate staleness deliberately elsewhere.', 'A financial balance requires serializable updates, while an analytics dashboard accepts delayed replicas with a visible freshness timestamp.', 'Spend coordination only on invariants that require it.'),
    lesson('Messaging decouples producers and consumers in time, but common brokers provide at-least-once delivery. Consumers must handle duplicates, reordering, poison messages, backpressure, and schema evolution.', 'Use transactional outbox for database-to-message reliability, idempotency keys, bounded retries, dead-letter handling, lag metrics, and replay procedures.', 'A payment service commits an outbox event with its transaction; the ledger consumer deduplicates by event ID before applying it.', 'At least once plus idempotency beats magical exactly once.'),
    lesson('Resilience contains partial failure through timeouts, retries with jitter, circuit breakers, bulkheads, queues, and graceful degradation. Each mechanism can amplify failure if unbounded or layered carelessly.', 'Set end-to-end deadlines, retry only safe transient operations, cap attempts, preserve capacity for recovery, and test dependency slowdown—not just hard failure.', 'When a fraud provider slows, payment requests use a short bounded retry and route eligible low-risk cases to manual review instead of exhausting every request thread.', 'Timeout first; retry safely; isolate capacity; degrade deliberately.'),
    lesson('Distributed observability reconstructs one user operation across services using correlated structured logs, metrics, traces, and events. Clock skew and sampling mean no single signal is complete.', 'Propagate trace and request IDs, measure golden signals and queue lag, record deployment versions, redact sensitive data, and retain enough evidence for replay and audit.', 'A trace shows a financial workflow waiting in a queue after one consumer deployment, confirmed by lag and version metrics.', 'Follow causality across request, message, dependency, and deployment.'),
    lesson('Threat modeling identifies assets, trust boundaries, actors, entry points, abuse cases, and mitigations before incidents. Security includes confidentiality, integrity, availability, and accountable recovery.', 'Authenticate workloads and users, authorize each resource action, encrypt transport and storage, minimize secrets and privileges, validate messages, rate-limit abuse, and test incident response.', 'A financial workflow signs provider webhooks, rejects replay, scopes service identity to one topic, and preserves an auditable immutable event record.', 'Asset, actor, boundary, abuse, control, detection, recovery.'),
  ],
};
